      "text": "To cover a square region exactly with 2-by-3 rectangles without gaps and overlaps, the goal is to find the smallest whole number of rectangles required such that no rectangles overlap and no space is left uncovered. We observe that if we divide the square into a grid of rectangles, the only viable option with the given dimensions is to divide it in two by the length of the rectangles, leading to each square becoming one 2x2 intersection point. \n\nHere's the process for the steps involved:\n\n1. The first consideration is to arrange the rectangles in such a manner that their combined length equals the perimeter of the square. For a 2 \\times 3 rectangle, their total length in unit is 2 + 3 = 5 units. If there are N of the rectangles, then their total length will be 5N units. Since the perimeter of the square is 4 times the length of one side, which is 4 square units for a unit square, it must be equal to 5N.\n\n2. Hence, to cover exactly 4 square units of area, the equation \\(5N = 4\\) needs to be satisfied. But since they could only be divided into whole sets, this only works when 5N is at least 4 but no higher. We end up finding that N cannot be a whole integer within these criteria. Thus, we look for the closest power of 2 that is less than the total area, allowing us to get to the minimum size N by dividing the square in half, which requires the minimum number of rectangles.\n\n3. Finally, arranging the 2x3 rectangles in a way that their total length matches the perimeter of the square (4 square units for a 1 by how many square units it would fit into a rectangle). After creating a rectangle, each square can only fit into the middle most square, which is a 2x2 rectangle, outside of the 1 by 1 unit square. So, combining 2 such rectangles gives us an area of 2 square units, fitting within the overall square area. \n\nThus, the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly is \\boxed{2}. \n\n**Note: The calculation above is based on the square of the longest side, which is 3, to form a crossing point with two two-unit-side rectangular pieces that cannot fit together.**\n\nThis method breaks down into simpler steps, and taking this real-world approach of covering areas in simplest terms allows us to find the solution.",
