      "text": "To determine the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly without extra overhangs and without gaps, we need to find a square that can be perfectly covered by these rectangles. This involves finding a square whose side length is a multiple of the dimensions of the rectangle (which is 2N x 3N for some integer N).\n\nFirst, note that since the rectangle's dimensions are 2N x 3N, it will only fit into the square if the side length of the square is a multiple of both 2N and 3N. Let's find the smallest such square.\n\nThe smallest possible dimensions of the rectangle that can cover the square are when the dimensions are multiples of both 2N and 3N, and thus must be in the format of 2Kx3K for some integer K. Therefore, the smallest square that can be covered by these rectangles will have side length 2Kx3K.\n\nWe need to find the smallest such square that can be fully covered with 2-by-3 rectangles without overlapping or leaving gaps. This means we need the square's side length to be a multiple of the least common multiple (LCM) of the dimensions 2 and 3.\n\nThe LCM of 2 and 3 is 6, so the smallest possible square that can be covered by these rectangles has a side length of 6N for some integer N. The area of this square is \\(6^2 \\times N^2 = 36N^2\\).\n\nNow, since each 2-by-3 rectangle covers an area of \\(6N \\times 3N = 18N^2\\), the number of rectangles needed to cover the square area is given by dividing the area of the square by the area of one rectangle:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{36N^2}{18N^2} = 2\n\\]\n\nSo, the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square exactly, without extra overhangs and without gaps, is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
